# Project orientation — {{PROJECT_TITLE}}

What we know about {{PHYSICAL_SYSTEM}} — the ONE background document for the whole project: the
governing physics, the conventions, the instrument, and the durable decisions, each as a section,
because anyone who needs one of them generally needs the others. Every campaign and session shares
it by reference (`../../PROJECT_ORIENTATION.md` from a campaign root); never copy any part of it
into a campaign. **Living document**: update it whenever a confirmed discovery changes the physical
understanding (the loop's UPDATE step in a campaign's `roles/worker.md`), and log each framework
revision under §Updates from discoveries so "what did earlier work believe" stays answerable.

> Notation: write all equations in math blocks (`$$ … $$` for display, `$…$` inline) with
> **precise, well-defined symbols and units**. Define every symbol the first time it appears.

## Standing constraints

**The operative section.** The campaign driver delivers everything under this heading into every
spawned worker and planner prompt, every cycle — a constraint recorded here is guaranteed present,
never merely pointed at. Keep it lean: the hard constraints a run must obey and the mistakes it
must not repeat; depth and derivations live in the sections below. When a bug keeps recurring, its
one-line guard belongs here.

### Physics inventory — what's modeled, what's not, and why
Every effect that could matter, tagged — so every omission is **deliberate and auditable**, and a
persistent model–data gap points straight at a parked effect (model-form error). Graduates from the
`frame-problem` framing artifact; re-check it whenever a discovery changes the physics.

| Effect | Tag | Why (number where "negligible") |
|---|---|---|
| {{EFFECT_1}} | accounted-for | <how it's modeled> |
| {{EFFECT_2}} | negligible-by-scale | <the estimate/ratio showing it's small here> |
| {{EFFECT_3}} | out-of-scope | <why outside this project> |
| {{EFFECT_4}} | deferred | <added when…> |
| {{EFFECT_5}} | unknown | <flagged risk — how we'll find out> |

